# platform.linux.fs.fat

mm.fs.fat on the Linux SDKs, over Linux's own FatFs R0.16: the linux-fatfs
library, which mm.build compiles from its C sources for a program that
reaches this provider. FAT on any 512-byte BlockDevice -- the storage-linux
board's emulated SD card through mm.sdcard.socket, a disk or image named by
the device map's storage entries through mm.fs's McuStorage, a device in a
test.

It is the platform.pico.fs.fat pattern, kept independent of it: this module
includes only the project-owned ABI header, fat-c.h, through its C++ shim,
and the C adapter beside it, adapter.c, includes FatFs and supplies its disk
I/O layer. The adapter keeps mm.fs's Volume rules in C, where the native
harness exercises them; this module maps statuses, paths, and formats across
the ABI and adapts a BlockDevice to the adapter's device record. Two volumes
may be attached at once, with eight open files and four open directories
between them.
